WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.780
Good morning, Hank. It's Tuesday.

00:00:00.780 --> 00:00:04.091
Let’s talk about the healthcare proposals
of Donald Trump and Hillary Clinton, but

00:00:04.091 --> 00:00:07.430
first I should say that if you’re interested
in healthcare or health in general, you should

00:00:07.430 --> 00:00:10.050
check out Health Care Triage, hosted by Dr.
Aaron Carroll.

00:00:10.050 --> 00:00:11.620
It’s an amazing show.

00:00:11.620 --> 00:00:14.720
Okay, to understand the candidates’ proposals,
we need to look at the current healthcare

00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:18.480
system and its problems, so journey back with
me to 2008.

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:21.260
At the time, most Americans got insurance
through private companies.

00:00:21.260 --> 00:00:25.020
There was a publicly funded program for the
elderly to get insurance called Medicare.

00:00:25.020 --> 00:00:28.680
Many low-income families got coverage through
a publicly funded program called Medicaid,

00:00:28.690 --> 00:00:32.050
and about 15% of Americans had no health insurance
at all.

00:00:32.050 --> 00:00:36.040
So, broadly speaking, back in 2008, our healthcare
system had three huge problems.

00:00:36.040 --> 00:00:38.820
The first was those 15% of people without
insurance.

00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:42.740
Many people who didn't work for big companies,
including me, literally could not get health

00:00:42.740 --> 00:00:47.239
insurance for any price because private insurers
could deny you based on pre-existing conditions.

00:00:47.239 --> 00:00:51.159
And for many other people, private insurance
plans were just too expensive to afford.

00:00:51.160 --> 00:00:55.220
And that brings me to our second big problem
back in 2008, which was that our health care

00:00:55.230 --> 00:00:57.110
just cost too much.

00:00:57.110 --> 00:01:01.290
We were spending 16% of our nation's total
economic output on healthcare.

00:01:01.290 --> 00:01:03.580
Most other rich nations spent under 10%.

00:01:03.590 --> 00:01:08.799
Like, our healthcare costs were so stupefyingly
high that in the United States, more tax dollars

00:01:08.799 --> 00:01:14.219
per capita went to healthcare spending than
Germany, Japan, the United Kingdom, or Canada.

00:01:14.219 --> 00:01:19.019
All those countries spending fewer tax dollars
per person on healthcare had universal,

00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:20.480
publicly funded healthcare.

00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:23.080
The United States had nothing close to that.

00:01:23.080 --> 00:01:27.359
Also, lastly, for all this money we were spending
not to insure everyone, we weren’t getting

00:01:27.359 --> 00:01:30.899
particularly good healthcare outcomes, like,
by almost any measure.

00:01:30.900 --> 00:01:35.100
From life expectancy to medical errors to
hospital admissions for preventable diseases,

00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:37.320
the United States was not near the top.

00:01:37.329 --> 00:01:41.829
So then comes the Affordable Care Act of 2010,
which was designed to address some of these

00:01:41.829 --> 00:01:44.460
problems, with the emphasis on the “some.”

00:01:44.469 --> 00:01:47.449
The ACA was primarily designed to get more
people health insurance.

00:01:47.449 --> 00:01:51.639
About half of the newly insured were supposed
to get Medicaid, which was expanded to include

00:01:51.639 --> 00:01:55.860
everyone making up to 138% of the poverty
line, and the other half were supposed to

00:01:55.860 --> 00:02:00.929
get insurance through exchanges, these marketplaces
where everyone can get health insurance regardless

00:02:00.929 --> 00:02:02.250
of pre-existing conditions.

00:02:02.250 --> 00:02:06.219
And the insurance would be affordable because
households making up to 400% of the poverty

00:02:06.219 --> 00:02:10.209
line would have their insurance costs subsidized
by the government on these exchanges.

00:02:10.209 --> 00:02:14.769
The ACA also sought to reduce spending by
lowering costs within Medicare, and it raised

00:02:14.769 --> 00:02:17.099
some new taxes, including one on medical devices.

00:02:17.099 --> 00:02:21.019
But it left the rest of the U.S. healthcare
system largely unchanged.

00:02:21.019 --> 00:02:22.019
So that was the basic idea.

00:02:22.019 --> 00:02:26.330
Now, the ACA has never been fully implemented
because a 2012 Supreme Court decision made

00:02:26.330 --> 00:02:30.989
the Medicaid expansion optional for states,
and nineteen states have so far declined to

00:02:30.989 --> 00:02:34.309
do it, which means that millions of poorer
Americans remain uninsured.

00:02:34.309 --> 00:02:38.880
That said, the ACA has been successful at
reducing the number of uninsured people.

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:40.920
Today, it’s around 9.1%.

00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:44.540
And it may have played a role, although experts
disagree about this, in the slowing growth

00:02:44.550 --> 00:02:46.500
of healthcare costs in the United States.

00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:51.400
For the twenty years before 2008, healthcare
costs in the United States rose by an average of 8%.

00:02:51.400 --> 00:02:54.920
In the last five years, they’ve risen by
an average of 5.5%.

00:02:54.920 --> 00:03:00.040
But, to be clear, costs are still rising faster
than inflation and median weekly earnings.

00:03:00.040 --> 00:03:04.379
Healthcare outcomes continue to lag behind,
and our healthcare system is still far, far

00:03:04.379 --> 00:03:08.859
more expensive than any other nation on earth,
although the gap with Europe has closed a bit.

00:03:08.860 --> 00:03:13.360
The ACA wasn’t really designed to address
those problems, and it hasn’t.

00:03:13.360 --> 00:03:14.420
And there are other problems, too.

00:03:14.420 --> 00:03:18.879
In the employer market, premiums and deductibles
are rising as employers cover on average a

00:03:18.879 --> 00:03:22.109
smaller percentage of healthcare expenses
than they did ten years ago.

00:03:22.109 --> 00:03:24.920
On the exchanges, some insurance companies
are offering fewer plans.

00:03:24.920 --> 00:03:27.900
And because companies are insurers are now
required to offer comprehensive coverage with

00:03:27.900 --> 00:03:32.189
no lifetime benefit limits, many people who
previously had bare bones insurance plans

00:03:32.189 --> 00:03:34.309
have seen their insurance costs go way up.

00:03:34.309 --> 00:03:38.340
These are all real problems, but it’s very
important to understand that the structural

00:03:38.349 --> 00:03:43.709
shortcomings of the United States healthcare
system are much, much older than Obamacare.

00:03:43.709 --> 00:03:47.960
The ACA is not the reason our healthcare costs
are so high, and we know that because they

00:03:47.960 --> 00:03:50.129
were very high before the ACA.

00:03:50.129 --> 00:03:53.279
So with all that in mind, let’s look at
both candidates’ healthcare reform proposals,

00:03:53.279 --> 00:03:58.339
using analysis from the RAND corporation,
which is nonpartisan and widely considered centrist.

00:03:58.340 --> 00:03:59.480
Let’s start with Trump’s plan.

00:03:59.480 --> 00:04:01.040
First, he would repeal the ACA.

00:04:01.040 --> 00:04:04.830
Now, Trump has said he’ll find ways to make
sure that private insurers continue offering

00:04:04.830 --> 00:04:09.109
plans to people with pre-existing conditions,
but he hasn’t said how this would be possible.

00:04:09.109 --> 00:04:11.930
Trump would also change the way Medicaid is
funded, to block grants.

00:04:11.930 --> 00:04:15.549
This would basically mean that states would
get to administer their own Medicaid programs,

00:04:15.549 --> 00:04:19.829
but most analyses see these block grants over time
involving lots of cuts.

00:04:19.829 --> 00:04:24.030
And lastly, Trump would allow insurance companies
to sell plans across state lines, and he would

00:04:24.030 --> 00:04:27.070
also make all health insurance premiums tax
deductible.

00:04:27.070 --> 00:04:31.820
But remember, these are deductions, not tax credits,
so they would mostly benefit people with high

00:04:31.830 --> 00:04:34.150
incomes for reasons explained in this video.

00:04:34.150 --> 00:04:37.900
Altogether, the RAND analysis concludes, I’m
just gonna quote here: “The Trump proposals

00:04:37.900 --> 00:04:42.460
decrease the number of insured, increase out-of-pocket
spending for consumers enrolled in individual

00:04:42.460 --> 00:04:45.789
market plans, and raise the federal deficit
compared to the ACA.”

00:04:45.789 --> 00:04:50.810
So, here’s how: Around 19.7 million people
would lose their insurance if the ACA were

00:04:50.810 --> 00:04:54.760
repealed, and Medicaid block grants would
probably increase the number of uninsured further.

00:04:54.760 --> 00:04:58.780
Sales of plans across state lines and tax
deductible premiums would get some people

00:04:58.780 --> 00:05:03.700
insured, but all in all, RAND concludes that
about 20.3 million fewer Americans would

00:05:03.700 --> 00:05:05.620
have health insurance under Trump’s plan.

00:05:05.620 --> 00:05:08.620
Out-of-pocket expenses would go up, according
to RAND, for two reasons.

00:05:08.620 --> 00:05:12.780
First, because the tax deductions offered
by the Trump plan are less generous than the

00:05:12.780 --> 00:05:15.180
tax credits offered by the ACA.

00:05:15.180 --> 00:05:18.490
And secondly, they estimate that sales of
plans across state lines would raise out-of-pocket

00:05:18.490 --> 00:05:22.210
expenses because there would be an increase
in the so-called bare bones plans.

00:05:22.210 --> 00:05:27.300
These are plans with high co-pays or deductibles,
or limits to annual or lifetime benefits.

00:05:27.300 --> 00:05:31.770
RAND estimates that the average annual out-of-pocket
expenses per person, including deductibles

00:05:31.770 --> 00:05:38.990
and co-pays and insurance premiums and everything,
would go from about $3200 per person to $5700 per person.

00:05:38.990 --> 00:05:42.759
And the deficit would go up, because with
the repeal of the ACA, the taxes it raised

00:05:42.759 --> 00:05:46.419
would also be repealed, as would the changes
in Medicare reimbursement.

00:05:46.420 --> 00:05:50.380
This would lower some taxes, but most of the
savings were in changes to Medicare, so all

00:05:50.390 --> 00:05:55.949
in all, under Trump’s plan, according to
RAND, the deficit would go up by about 5.8 billion dollars.

00:05:55.949 --> 00:05:59.970
And I should add that all the nonpartisan
analyses I could find agreed on all three fronts:

00:05:59.970 --> 00:06:04.129
that Trump’s plan would reduce the
number of insured people, increase out-of-pocket

00:06:04.129 --> 00:06:05.880
costs, and increase the deficit.

00:06:05.880 --> 00:06:07.720
Okay, let’s move on to Hillary Clinton’s
proposals.

00:06:07.720 --> 00:06:10.940
Clinton would amend the ACA in three big ways.

00:06:10.940 --> 00:06:16.240
First she would introduce a $2500 tax credit,
or $5000 for married couples filing jointly,

00:06:16.240 --> 00:06:20.160
that could be applied to healthcare expenses
over 5% of income.

00:06:20.160 --> 00:06:24.340
So if you’re a single person making $50,000
a year and you pay $5000 a year in health

00:06:24.340 --> 00:06:27.440
insurance premiums, you would get a $2500
tax credit.

00:06:27.440 --> 00:06:31.660
Because this is a tax credit and not a deduction,
it would be available to everyone, not just

00:06:31.660 --> 00:06:34.430
high-income households, but that also means
it would be more expensive.

00:06:34.430 --> 00:06:38.220
Secondly, Clinton would reduce the marketplace
premium maximum.

00:06:38.220 --> 00:06:39.500
I know this is a little confusing.

00:06:39.509 --> 00:06:43.500
Healthcare policy makes tax policy look like
a pleasant walk in the park.

00:06:43.500 --> 00:06:47.550
But basically, right now the cost of health
insurance premiums on the marketplace exchanges is

00:06:47.550 --> 00:06:50.660
effectively capped at 9.6% of income.

00:06:50.660 --> 00:06:54.840
To not benefit from the cap, a family of four
has to make $96,000 a year or more.

00:06:54.840 --> 00:07:00.860
Basically, Clinton’s plan would lower the
cap from 9.6% to 8.5% for most households.

00:07:00.860 --> 00:07:02.860
But of course, that program would also cost
money.

00:07:02.860 --> 00:07:07.460
And then lastly, and maybe most interestingly,
Clinton would offer a public insurance option

00:07:07.470 --> 00:07:08.850
on the exchanges.

00:07:08.850 --> 00:07:12.599
This would allow anyone, not just seniors
and low-income people to get their insurance

00:07:12.599 --> 00:07:13.610
from the government.

00:07:13.610 --> 00:07:17.129
RAND hasn’t yet updates its analysis of
the public option, but basically, it would

00:07:17.129 --> 00:07:20.860
probably be cheaper than private insurance
because it would share administrative costs

00:07:20.860 --> 00:07:25.180
with Medicare, and so it would slightly reduce
the deficit by reducing healthcare subsidies.

00:07:25.180 --> 00:07:28.340
So all in all, according to RAND, I’m just
gonna quote again: “All of the policies

00:07:28.340 --> 00:07:32.611
considered increase the number of insured
people and reduce consumers’ out-of-pocket

00:07:32.611 --> 00:07:33.979
spending on health care.”

00:07:33.979 --> 00:07:38.520
RAND estimates that the Clinton tax credit
alone would lead to 9.6 million more people

00:07:38.520 --> 00:07:42.730
getting health insurance, and that households
of all income levels would see their out-of-pocket

00:07:42.730 --> 00:07:44.530
healthcare expenses go down.

00:07:44.530 --> 00:07:47.360
The biggest reduction in healthcare costs
would be for families making between thirty

00:07:47.360 --> 00:07:50.130
and sixty thousand dollars a year, but they
would go down for everyone.

00:07:50.130 --> 00:07:54.180
But of course these problems would also increase
the federal deficit by what RAND estimates

00:07:54.180 --> 00:07:55.800
to be ninety billion dollars.

00:07:55.800 --> 00:07:56.920
So that is RAND’s summary.

00:07:56.920 --> 00:08:00.520
Trump’s plan would lead to significantly
higher out-of-pocket costs, fewer insured

00:08:00.520 --> 00:08:02.850
people, and a modestly higher budget deficit.

00:08:02.850 --> 00:08:07.070
Clinton’s plan would insure more people,
lower out-of-pocket costs, but raise the deficit more.

00:08:07.070 --> 00:08:10.720
But of course you have to consider those deficits
in the broader context of the candidates’

00:08:10.729 --> 00:08:11.819
budget and tax proposals.

00:08:11.819 --> 00:08:15.980
And to be clear, overall, under Trump’s
proposals, the budget deficit would be trillions

00:08:15.980 --> 00:08:17.930
of dollars more than under Clinton’s proposals.

00:08:17.930 --> 00:08:22.349
The other question, of course, is will either
of these proposals significantly reduce the

00:08:22.349 --> 00:08:24.429
overall cost of U.S. healthcare?

00:08:24.429 --> 00:08:26.520
And the answer is probably not.

00:08:26.520 --> 00:08:29.789
The Trump campaign will argue that selling
policies across state lines will increase

00:08:29.789 --> 00:08:32.069
competition, but that is deeply contested.

00:08:32.069 --> 00:08:35.700
And also, even in the rosiest projections,
it wouldn’t decrease spending much.

00:08:35.710 --> 00:08:39.260
The Clinton campaign, meanwhile, will say
that a public option would put real price

00:08:39.260 --> 00:08:40.790
pressure on private insurers.

00:08:40.790 --> 00:08:43.850
And it might, but I’ll put a link in the
dooblydoo explaining why a single-payer healthcare

00:08:43.850 --> 00:08:46.780
system would not magically fix the U.S.’s
problems.

00:08:46.790 --> 00:08:51.820
The truth is, this is a complicated, multifaceted
problem, and anyone trying to sell you simple

00:08:51.820 --> 00:08:54.840
solutions probably isn’t telling you the
whole story.

00:08:54.840 --> 00:08:56.160
And that includes me, by the way.

00:08:56.160 --> 00:08:57.920
This is definitely only an introduction.

00:08:57.920 --> 00:09:01.570
If you want to learn much more about healthcare
policy and health insurance and different

00:09:01.570 --> 00:09:04.270
strategies for dealing with it, check out
Health Care Triage.

00:09:04.270 --> 00:09:07.460
They’ve got a great video out now about
Medicaid’s return on investment, and next

00:09:07.460 --> 00:09:10.900
week, they’re have a much more comprehensive
analysis of the candidates’ healthcare plans,

00:09:10.900 --> 00:09:12.320
so subscribe to them.

00:09:12.330 --> 00:09:15.790
You can find sources, as well as information
about how to vote in the dooblydoo below.

00:09:15.790 --> 00:09:19.780
Hank, I will see you on Friday, unless you
have a child in the interim.

00:09:19.780 --> 00:09:23.440
End screen, Hank always says I need an end
screen.

00:09:23.440 --> 00:09:27.370
So I put some videos here about health insurance,
why you need it, what the healthcare system

00:09:27.370 --> 00:09:30.480
in the United States is like, why it’s so
incredibly complicated.

00:09:30.480 --> 00:09:35.480
Also, if you’re looking for a hot slice
of tax policy, that video is just one click away,

00:09:35.480 --> 00:09:38.740
and Rosianna drew you a bunny to cheer
you up.

00:09:38.740 --> 00:09:39.660
Thanks, Rosianna.

